Description: Autoencoding random forests (RFAEs) provide a method to
    autoencode data using random forests, which involves projecting the
    data to a latent feature space of chosen dimensionality (usually a
    lower dimension), and then decoding the latent representations back
    into the input space. The encoding stage is useful for feature
    engineering and data visualisation tasks, akin to how principal
    component analysis (PCA) is used , and the decoding stage is useful
    for compression and denoising tasks. At its core, RFAE is a
    post-processing pipeline on a trained random forest model. This means
    that it can accept any trained RF of ranger object type: RF, URF or
    ARFs. Because of this, it inherits RFs' robust performance and
    capacity to seamlessly handle mixed-type tabular data. For more
    details, see Vu et al. (2025) <https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21441>.
